200 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E (   The proposed sorting mechanism integrates exceptional reliability, user-friendly sorting, and low maintenance costs. Its design employs a two-tiered principle, operated by merely two servo drives that manage three guide doors. The process initiates in the receiving area, where, following the scanning and categorization of the waste, the item is deposited into the sorting channel. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
201 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 109 0 R
/Pg 16 0 R
/A [433 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
202 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (7)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
203 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (   The upper level features the first door, which is controlled by an independent servo drive that alters its position autonomously, directing the flow to either the left or right. The lower level consists of a pair of interconnected doors, actuated by a second servo drive through a straightforward and durable lever system. The synchronized movement of the lower doors enables precise redirection of the descending object to the intended section. The combination of the two positions of the upper door and the two positions of the lower pair results in precisely four distinct movement trajectories. This configuration ensures that any discarded item will land accurately and without delay in one of the four designated compartments. The limited number of moving electric drives and lever-based kinematics significantly diminish the likelihood of malfunctions and streamline operation. Consequently, this waste distribution method is remarkably efficient, practical, and poised for implementation in real-world scenarios.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
204 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 109 0 R
/Pg 16 0 R
/A [434 0 R 0]
/K [3]
>>
endobj
